# Guest kernel deltas for kamaji's microVM backend (R605-F14 / W325 §5).
#
# Merged over base-x86_64-6.1.config — Firecracker's own CI guest config, vendored
# verbatim — and then run through `make olddefconfig` by build-guest-image.sh,
# which afterwards re-asserts REQUIRED_SYMBOLS so a silently-dropped symbol fails
# the build rather than producing an image that will not boot.
#
# ── Why the base is Firecracker's config and not one of ours ──────────────────
#
# The first version of this file was a from-nothing config over `make
# tinyconfig`, on the reasoning that a guest booting one job needs a hundredth of
# a distro kernel. It built, and it did not boot:
#
#   virtio_blk virtio0: 1/0/0 default/read/poll queues
#   virtio_blk: probe of virtio0 failed with error -22
#   VFS: Cannot open root device "vda" or unknown-block(0,0): error -6
#   Kernel panic - not syncing: VFS: Unable to mount root fs
#
# What that was bisected to, measured on us-west-003 with firecracker v1.16.1:
# taking Firecracker's own config and turning off **CONFIG_PCI alone** reproduces
# it exactly. A Firecracker guest has no PCI bus and kamaji passes `pci=off`, so
# switching PCI off is the obvious economy, and it silently breaks the virtio-MMIO
# block device the guest boots from.
#
# The mechanism is NOT established. The only interrupt-related symbols lost when
# PCI goes off are CONFIG_GENERIC_MSI_IRQ and CONFIG_GENERIC_MSI_IRQ_DOMAIN
# (both `select`ed by PCI_MSI), which makes them the candidates — but that was
# not confirmed, and neither `select` can be turned back on from a fragment, so
# confirming it means patching Kconfig. Recorded as a candidate, not a cause.
#
# The lesson is not "keep that one symbol". It is that a guest kernel config is a
# contract with a specific VMM's emulated hardware, that contract is documented
# nowhere as a symbol list, and the only party who tests it is the VMM's own CI.
# So the base is theirs, this file is the delta, and the delta is short enough to
# read. The build script's REQUIRED_SYMBOLS check names CONFIG_PCI explicitly for
# the same reason: the next person to have this good idea gets a build failure
# instead of a boot failure.
#
# ── The delta ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
#
# Exactly one symbol, and it is load-bearing. `/proc/cmdline` inside a guest
# booted by firecracker v1.16.1 reads:
#
#   console=ttyS0 reboot=k panic=1 pci=off i8042.noaux i8042.nomux pci=off \
#     root=/dev/vda ro virtio_mmio.device=4K@0xc0001000:5 \
#     virtio_mmio.device=4K@0xc0002000:6
#
# — measured, not read from a doc. Two things in it are worth knowing. Firecracker
# appends `root=/dev/vda ro` itself, from the drive marked `is_root_device`, which
# is why kamaji's `vmm_config` does not and must not. And it advertises its
# virtio devices as `virtio_mmio.device=` command-line arguments rather than
# through ACPI, which the guest kernel can only act on with the symbol below.
# Without it the devices are never registered at all and the guest panics with no
# root filesystem — the same visible failure as the ACPI problem above, from an
# unrelated cause.
CONFIG_VIRTIO_MMIO_CMDLINE_DEVICES=y
